    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>German rule began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a>' treaties with mainland chieftains and an imperial charter of 1885, and was imposed on the coast when Bismarck's warships forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar to accept the German claims.</li><li>The conquest was marked by widespread atrocities, and the Maji Maji Rebellion of 1905 was crushed by measures to create a famine that may have cost as many as 300,000 lives.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the colony was divided: Tanganyika went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Ruanda and Urundi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kionga-triangle">Kionga Triangle</a> to Portugal.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> never formally abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in German East Africa: in 1914 officials considered a ban but did not make one, since they did not think it financially possible to compensate the owners.</p></aside>
    </section>

