    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-peters.jpg" alt="Carl Peters" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R30019,_Dr._Carl_Peters.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony began with the treaties that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a>, an adventurer and the founder of the Society for German Colonization, signed with several chieftains on the mainland opposite Zanzibar. On 3 March 1885 the German government announced an imperial charter, signed by Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> on 27 February, which granted Peters' company a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in the African Great Lakes region. Peters' specialists then explored south to the Rufiji River and north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witu">Witu</a>, near Lamu. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar protested that he ruled the mainland as well as the islands; Bismarck sent five warships, which arrived on 7 August 1885 and trained their guns on the Sultan's palace, and the Sultan was forced to accept the German claims outside a coastal strip of 16 kilometres.</p><p>In November 1886 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> agreed to respect the Sultan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over his islands and that strip, and divided the mainland between themselves into spheres of interest; the Sultan had no option but to agree. German rule was established quickly over Bagamoyo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> and Kilwa. The colony proper was organised when the German military was called in to put down a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa-company">German East Africa Company</a>, the Abushiri Revolt of 1888, crushed with British help the following year. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty">Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</a> of 1890 settled the border with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> Protectorate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivory-trade.jpg" alt="Ivory trade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory trade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fang_traders_with_ivory.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown via Fleming H. Revell Company, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early years hunting and gathering remained the basis of the export economy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> were the main exports. European-owned plantations multiplied but many proved unprofitable in unstable world markets; only sisal, grown on over 40,000 hectares, gave the large estates a reliable income. Two million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> trees were planted, rubber covered 81,000 hectares and there were large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantations. About 70,000 Africans worked on the plantations; local officials often colluded with landowners in forcing them to work, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> had banned forced labour, and labour migration devastated 'labour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>' such as Unyamwezi and Usukuma. Gold was found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> in 1894, and the Sekenke Gold Mine opened in 1909.</p><p>The Germans never formally abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. New enslavement and the commercial slave trade were banned in 1901 and all children born after 1905 were born free, but private sales went on: thousands of slaves, mostly women, were sold in 1911–1914, while thousands of others bought their freedom or left their enslavers. On the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the colony, the most populous of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, had more than 7.5 million inhabitants, about 30% of them Muslim, against around 10,000 Europeans, of whom 3,579 were Germans.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck.jpg" alt="Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck">Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Paul_von_Lettow-Vorbeck_LCCN2014719610b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photoatelier Hermann Noack, Berlin; cropped, retouched and resolution reduced by Jossi2, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>German expansion was carried out by military groups such as the Wissmann Truppe, African soldiers under German commanders armed with modern weapons, who committed widespread atrocities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a> earned the name 'Mkono-wa-damu', 'Man with the blood-stained hands', for the villages slaughtered in his conquests; rape, floggings and torture with iron rods were common, and other European powers called the German colonies the 'flogging colonies'. The Hehe under Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mkwawa">Mkwawa</a> resisted from 1891 to 1894 and were defeated because rival peoples supported the Germans; after years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war Mkwawa, cornered, killed himself in 1898. The Maji Maji Rebellion of 1905 was put down by Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-adolf-von-gotzen">Gustav Adolf von Götzen</a>, who ordered measures to create a famine; it may have cost as many as 300,000 lives.</p><p>On 1 January 1914 the Schutztruppe garrisons numbered 110 German officers, 126 non-commissioned officers and 2,472 Askari. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck">Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck</a> led 3,500 Europeans and 12,000 Askari and porters against a British army of 40,000, at times under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a>. At the Battle of Tanga in November 1914 his forces defeated a British force more than eight times larger, and his guerrilla war tied down British resources and inflicted more than 10,000 casualties. The light cruiser SMS Königsberg was scuttled in the Rufiji delta in July 1915, and her guns joined the land forces.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;German East Africa&#8221; cites where it covers World War I:</p><ul><li>Edwin P. Hoyt, Guerilla: Colonel von Lettow-Vorbeck and Germany's East African Empire (Macmillan, 1981)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii.jpg" alt="Wilhelm II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_II_of_Germany_-_1902_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Studio of Thomas Heinrich Voigt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a> was the company's administrator from 27 May 1885 to 8 February 1888. After the Abushiri Revolt the Reich took over: Hermann Wissmann served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reichskommissar</a> from 1888 to 1891, and from 1891 imperial governors ruled the colony, from Julius von Soden to the last, Heinrich Schnee, from 1912 to 1918. Peters, named Reichskommissar for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Kilimanjaro</a> Region in 1891 under Soden, was recalled in 1892 and in 1897, after three investigations, deprived of his commission, his title and his pension; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> nevertheless gave him the title of 'retired Reichskommissar' in 1905.</p><p>The administration relied heavily on African chiefs to keep order and collect taxes, and the labour ordinances issued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> were largely ignored in the interior. The scandals of corruption and brutality that followed the Maji Maji war led Chancellor Bernhard von Bülow to appoint Bernhard Dernburg in 1907 to reform the colonial administration, and under Albrecht von Rechenberg, governor from 1906 to 1912, policy turned to African small-holder farming, for which railways were built. The government ran elementary, secondary and vocational schools for Africans. Its records from 1884 to 1917, held by the Tanzania National Archives, were inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Register</a> in 1997.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>German East Africa covered 994,996 square kilometres, nearly three times the area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and took in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>, Rwanda, mainland Tanzania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kionga-triangle">Kionga Triangle</a>. The agreements of 1886 and 1890 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> drew the line that became the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and Tanzania, surveyed from the sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Mount Kilimanjaro</a> in 1892 and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> twelve years later. The Central Railroad, 1,247 kilometres long, linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morogoro">Morogoro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a> and Kigoma, and harbours were fitted with electric cranes, rail access and warehouses; in 1912 Dar es Salaam and Tanga received 356 steamers and over 1,000 coastal vessels. Rainer Tetzlaff nevertheless concluded that the colony never achieved any real significance for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, as a settlement, a supplier of raw materials or a market.</p><p>For its African peoples the colony meant conquest, forced labour and the famine of the Maji Maji war. Some of its institutions outlasted it: in 1924 the visiting Phelps-Stokes Commission judged that the German schools had met standards unmatched in tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> word for school, shule, comes from the German Schule. German spellings of local names, such as 'Kilimandscharo', stayed in use for a while. The Askari who fought in the East African campaign later received pensions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-milner"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-milner.jpg" alt="Alfred Milner" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-milner">Alfred Milner</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Milner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> ended the colony. Outnumbered and short of supplies after forces from the Belgian Congo had attacked from the west, in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>, Lettow-Vorbeck abandoned the colony, withdrew south into Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and then into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, and agreed to a ceasefire on hearing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> concluded three days earlier. A smaller campaign on the southern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> in 1914 and 1915 had pitted a makeshift British and Belgian flotilla against the garrison at Bismarckburg. In Germany Lettow-Vorbeck was acclaimed as a hero and his Schutztruppe celebrated as the only German colonial force not defeated in open combat, though it often retreated when outnumbered.</p><p>On 7 May 1919 the Supreme Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> awarded all of German East Africa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>'s strenuous objections. By the Anglo-Belgian agreement of 30 May 1919, negotiated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-milner">Alfred Milner</a> and Pierre Orts, Britain ceded the north-western districts of Ruanda and Urundi to Belgium; on 12 July the Commission on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">Mandates</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kionga-triangle">Kionga Triangle</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruvuma-river">Rovuma River</a> to Portugal, reasoning that Germany had virtually forced Portugal to cede it in 1894. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> took effect on 10 January 1920, when the territory passed to the three powers and the British part took the name Tanganyika.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>German East Africa grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a>' treaties and an imperial charter of 1885 into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s largest and most populous colony. Its conquest, forced labour and the famine that crushed the Maji Maji Rebellion cost hundreds of thousands of lives; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and Portugal.</em></p>

